@meta
id: 18-13
title: Read a file in
chapter: 18
type: lesson
skills: edit-file, wildmenu
par: 59
minutes: 4
vscode: open the other file, select all, copy, come back, paste
vimrc: set wildmenu wildmode=longest:full,full
open: README.md

@brief
The README has an "Error codes" section with an empty `json` code
block. It should contain the current contents of
`src/configuration/errors/default.json`, exactly as they are in that
file.

Do not open the JSON, copy it and paste it. `:r path` reads a file and
inserts its lines below the cursor line, in place, without leaving the
buffer. Put the cursor on the opening fence line and read the file in.
The JSON file itself must not change.

@keys
| `:r path` | read a file in, below the current line |
| `:0r path` | read it in above the first line |
| `:r %:h/other` | modifiers work here too |

@hints
Get the cursor onto the line that reads ```` ```json ````, then `:r`
and the path. Tab completes it.
--
`/json` Enter finds the fence. The path is
`src/configuration/errors/default.json`; `s<Tab>c<Tab>e<Tab>d<Tab>`
spells it.
--
`/json` Enter, `:r src/configuration/errors/default.json` Enter, `:wq`.

@solution
    /json<Enter> :r s<Tab>c<Tab>e<Tab>d<Tab><Enter> :wq<Enter>

`:r` is the underrated one. Pulling a fixture into a test, a snippet
into a doc, a licence header into a new file: every time the other file
is going in whole, `:r` is one command where copy and paste is four
buffer switches. `:0r` puts it at the very top, which is what a header
wants.

Chapter 26 gives `:r` a second life: `:r !command` reads a shell
command's output in the same way.

@start README.md
# Orders service

Handles orders, customers and refunds for the storefront.

## Error codes

```json
```

Each code maps to an HTTP status. `sentry: true` means an alert fires.

@expected README.md
# Orders service

Handles orders, customers and refunds for the storefront.

## Error codes

```json
{
  "ORDER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED": { "statusCode": 409, "sentry": false },
  "CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "REFUND_FAILED": { "statusCode": 502, "sentry": true }
}
```

Each code maps to an HTTP status. `sentry: true` means an alert fires.

@start src/configuration/errors/default.json
{
  "ORDER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED": { "statusCode": 409, "sentry": false },
  "CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "REFUND_FAILED": { "statusCode": 502, "sentry": true }
}

@start src/configuration/registry.ts
import { container } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from '../domain/order/order.repository';
import { OrderProvider } from '../domain/order/order.provider';

container.register('OrderRepository', { useClass: OrderRepository });
container.register('OrderProvider', { useClass: OrderProvider });
